{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-93672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93672" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-93672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+6"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93672" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =2$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-8\\alpha =4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-93669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}+4"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=-4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+16\\alpha =-4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-7904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-2"] ["math" "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7904" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=12.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =2$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-6\\alpha =12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7896" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-7896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}+2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}+3"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7896" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=20\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+5\\alpha =20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-93668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+5"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=10"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=2"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=3x+2"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=-5x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha -5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x+(-4\\alpha -5\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-64285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-x+4"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-x-\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2x-10"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=\\frac{x}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64285" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=x-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-16y=8x-32.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-32\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-16\\alpha x-16\\beta =8x-32.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-16\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -16\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-93812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-2x+4"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-x+1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4"] ["math" "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)-\\frac{x}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=18x+3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3,$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(-\\alpha -6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -\\alpha -6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-93811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+3"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=3x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+1\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta )=3x+1.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-x-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-7897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-2x+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-2x+3"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-x-1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-x}-x+2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-x}-x-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-x-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=32x+16.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha +16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n16\\alpha x+(8\\alpha +16\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha =32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 8\\alpha +16\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-7902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-4"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-x"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}+2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=24{x}^{2}+4x+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =-3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +7(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(14\\alpha +12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha =24\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 14\\alpha +12\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-93830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}+2x+4"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}-{x}^{2}+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}+{x}^{2}-2x+4"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-{x}^{2}+2x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-93827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-2{x}^{2}+4x+1"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}+2x-1"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-93827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=25{x}^{2}+5x-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-7903" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7903" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+2x-1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+1"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7903" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-64206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+2x+3"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+3x-6"] ["math" "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}-x+6"] ["math" "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+x+6"] ["math" "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-3x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-64206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+2x+3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-77763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-77763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\beta -6\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\beta -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =6,\\,\\gamma =6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-77763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-77763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-77763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4x+6"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+3x-4"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x+8"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-77763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{4}{3},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=0,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{4}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, $A=1.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(B-3A){e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nB-3A=5.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-7901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-7901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-4Bx{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4A+B & =0 \\\\ -4(1)+B & =0 \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-94258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y={e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-1 & =0 \\\\ P & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}[(2Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-Q-2P)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]-2x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q-P+1 & =4 \\\\ 2Q-1+1 & =4 \\\\ Q & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-94329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=8x-4\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}-2x+1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=8x-4,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+1 & =4 \\\\ A+B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+2B{e}^{2x}-2,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2A+2B-2 & =0 \\\\ A-B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=3 \\\\ A-B=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-94342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{e}^{-2x}+3{e}^{2x}-2x+1"] ["math" "y=2{e}^{-2x}-2x+1"] ["math" "y=3{e}^{2x}-2x+1"] ["math" "y=4{e}^{-2x}-{e}^{2x}-2x+1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-94342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-8034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=13{x}^{2}+15x-24,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-8034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-8034" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "495b3e8ab551dac2922bc6b9100c40e9d980f3e97d840e4df72077fa0d1e6a5f"} {"question" "q-8034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-8034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x-1"] ["math" "y={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "q-8034" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/state.json" "file_sha256" "b5411cdd29a278a35e69eaece66ebe2781cb8eb406397af84798ce3fe070666f"} {"question" "e-6721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} - \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y = 6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6721.json" "file_sha256" "90102f82363fe2d0c485e92a3e7ba9eccaf520c4d74051064adfffb63a4f6507"} {"question" "e-6721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = -1, 2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ {- x} + B e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-0-2\\alpha =6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = -3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6721.json" "file_sha256" "90102f82363fe2d0c485e92a3e7ba9eccaf520c4d74051064adfffb63a4f6507"} {"question" "e-6607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} -4 \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} +4 y = 8x + 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6607.json" "file_sha256" "fbeea5a85d1b85dfdde9348a357f3662c05f1fb3db545208516ea300d56926b2"} {"question" "e-6607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6607.json" "file_sha256" "fbeea5a85d1b85dfdde9348a357f3662c05f1fb3db545208516ea300d56926b2"} {"question" "e-6608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6608.json" "file_sha256" "f3671779726174b74f2bf4cbfdbed96543ac968a8d0955173d7e02cd1732036a"} {"question" "e-6608" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6608.json" "file_sha256" "f3671779726174b74f2bf4cbfdbed96543ac968a8d0955173d7e02cd1732036a"} {"question" "e-6786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x} - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3,\n\\qquad y(0) =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\ \\ y'(0) = 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6786.json" "file_sha256" "0329d5ec8d55150b059effaf1e5091c1872790e210509de32c13cf636014761e"} {"question" "e-6786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065049/example-6786.json" "file_sha256" "0329d5ec8d55150b059effaf1e5091c1872790e210509de32c13cf636014761e"}] "usage" {"e-6607" [] "q-7902" [] "e-6721" [] "e-6786" [] "q-7903" [] "q-7901" [] "q-64206" [] "e-6608" [] "q-8034" []} "basis" 2133 "retractions" [[17592186088308 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 8\\frac{dy}{dx} + 16y = 32x + 16$."] [17592186088309 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 10\\frac{dy}{dx} + 25y = 25x^{2} + 5x - 3$."] [17592186088165 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 4\\frac{dy}{dx} - 12y = 12x^{2} - 32x - 30$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{2x} + Be^{-6x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation."] [17592186088307 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 9y = 3, y(0) = \\frac{4}{3}, y^{′}(0) = 5$."] [17592186088325 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 26y = 13x^{2} + 15x - 24, y(0) = 2, y^{′}(0) = \\frac{5}{2}$."] [17592186087548 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} - \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y = 6$."] [17592186087548 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1, 2\n$$\n\nso the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ {-x} + B e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0$, which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0$, or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n0 - 0 - 2α = 6⟹α =-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = -3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{-x} + Be^{2x} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087540 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} -4 \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} +4 y = 8x + 4$."] [17592186087540 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2\n$$\n\nso the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ {2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1$, a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1$. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 - 4α + 4(αx + β) &= 8x + 4 \\\\\n4αx + (4β - 4α) &= 8x + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{4α = 8\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients ofx) \\\\ 4β - 4α = 4\\end{vmatrix}(equating the constants)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=2, \\beta=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{2x} + Bxe^{2x} + 2x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087541 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$y'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3$."] [17592186087541 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1,-2\n$$\n\nso the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2$, a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2$. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x +\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, and $\\gamma$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2α + (2αx + β) - 2(αx^{2} + βx + γ) &= x^{2} + 5x - 3 \\\\\n- 2αx^{2} + (2α - 2β)x + (2α + β - 2γ) &= x^{2} + 5x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{-2α = 1\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients ofx^{2}) \\\\ 2α - 2β = 5\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients ofx); 2α + β - 2γ =-3 \\mid (equating the constants)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =-\\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Be^{-2x} - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} - 3x - \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087552 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$y'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3$\n\nhas the general solution\n$y = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x} - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$y'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3, \\qquad y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\ \\ y'(0) = 0$."] [17592186087552 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n$A+B -\\dfrac{1}{2} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3$,\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n$A-2B -3 = 0$.\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{A + B = 0; A - 2B = 3\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x} - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085922 17592186085923 17592186085924 17592186085925 17592186085926 17592186086773 17592186086774 17592186086775 17592186086776 17592186086777 17592186086779 17592186086780 17592186086781 17592186086782 17592186086783 17592186086785 17592186086786 17592186086787 17592186086788 17592186086789 17592186086881 17592186086882 17592186086883 17592186086884 17592186086885] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085921 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085921 :answer-field/id] [17592186085921 :answer-field/key] [17592186085921 :answer-field/type] [17592186086772 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086772 :answer-field/id] [17592186086772 :answer-field/key] [17592186086772 :answer-field/type] [17592186086778 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086778 :answer-field/id] [17592186086778 :answer-field/key] [17592186086778 :answer-field/type] [17592186086784 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086784 :answer-field/id] [17592186086784 :answer-field/key] [17592186